          "distinct": true,
          "id": "c4b28ffa70097a110c41d8c9dd47a19434cd8d05",
          "message": "feat: load-aware DP request routing for CoreManager (#1638)\n\n* feat: add load-aware DP request routing to CoreManager\n\nReplace CoreManager's pure round-robin DP dispatch with a configurable\nload-balancing strategy (default: least_requests) to cut DP-lockstep dummy\nbatches caused by uneven request distribution across ranks.\n\n- --dp-load-balance {round_robin,least_requests,least_tokens}\n  (Config.dp_load_balance); no effect when data_parallel_size == 1\n- least_requests (default): fewest in-flight requests, ties broken by the\n  lighter in-flight prompt-token load, then a rotating cursor\n- least_tokens: lowest combined load (prompt_tokens + ATOM_DP_LB_REQ_EQUIV * reqs)\n- in-flight load charged on dispatch, released on finish/abort (idempotent,\n  _lb_lock-guarded); explicit data_parallel_rank hints validated up front so a\n  bad hint rejects the batch before any charge; send failure rolls back\n  undispatched ranks\n- extract EngineCoreRequestType into engine_core_protocol.py so CoreManager\n  imports it without the heavy engine_core -> async_proc -> aiter chain; CPU-only\n  unit tests import it directly with no sys.modules stub\n- one merged INFO line per add: per-rank delta + resulting in-flight distribution\n\nBenchmarked on DeepSeek-V4-Pro TBO+DPA tp8 8k/1k c1024 (prompt x4): neutral vs\noriginal round-robin (40.9k vs 41.1k tok/s, within run-to-run noise), balanced\n[128 x 8] routing. 22 new unit tests; full suite 606 passed.\n\n* feat: show padded graph bs in decode record_function label\n\nWhen a decode step replays a CUDAGraph captured for a padded batch size, the\nlabel now shows bs=<real>/<graph> (e.g. bs=117/128) so the padding is visible in\nthe trace; plain bs=<n> is kept when the graph bs equals the real batch.\n\n- build_run_label gains an optional graph_bs arg; the slash form is emitted only\n  when graph_bs > bs (CUDAGraph path). ModelRunner.run_model passes\n  context.graph_bs on the CUDAGraph path only (None on the eager path)\n- parse_trace.py is unaffected: re.search(r\"bs=(\\d+)\") still reads the real\n  batch (117) from \"bs=117/128\" (regression test added)\n- run_labels docstring + capture-trace SKILL.md updated",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-19T19:25:52+08:00",
